content.text:
One behavioral finding from the smoke: the "fresh run" semantics. After a failed phase-2 run, a new `[factory] phase 1/8 capture: retry` line gives phases `[active, failed]` — the failed phase-2 status from the previous run persists.

Look at `foldStatusLine`:
```js
if (n === 1 && !state.running && phases[0].status !== 'pending') {
    phases = emptyProjectionState().phases;
}
```
The reset only fires when `!state.running`. But after a **failed** phase, `running` stays `true` (by design: "failed run stays running until phase 8 or a fresh run" — hmm, actually let me re-read the fold):

```js
const final = phases[PHASES.length - 1].status;
return {
    running: final !== 'done' && final !== 'failed' && final !== 'skipped',
    ...
};
```

`running` is computed ONLY from phase 8's status! A failed phase 2 leaves phase 8 `pending` → running stays true. So the fresh-run reset (gated on `!state.running`) never triggers after a mid-run failure — the failed status of phase 2 persists into the "new run".

Is that a real-world problem? Scenario: a run fails at phase 2 (agent reports failure, stops). The user fixes things and runs `/factory` again. The new run's phase-1 line should reset the board. With the current logic, `state.running` is `true` (since phase 8 is still pending), so no reset — the new run inherits the stale failed phase-2 row until the new phase-2 line overwrites it. Visually: new run active at phase 1, phase 2 still shows the OLD failure text. Then when the new run reaches phase 2, it updates. Rows 3-8 keep old states too — but wait, entering phase n closes earlier phases as done; the stale `failed` phase-2 would be closed as done when phase 3 starts... Actually the closure loop sets `active|pending → done` but leaves `failed` as `failed`! So a stale failure would persist through the whole new run. That's a genuine fold bug for the fresh-run story.

But hold on — is the reset condition sensible otherwise? For a COMPLETED run (phase 8 done), running=false → a new phase-1 line resets ✓. For a failed/skipped-at-8 run, running=false → reset ✓. Only mid-run-abandoned failures keep running=true.

What's the right semantic? A phase-1 status line ALWAYS begins a new run (the prompt instructs the agent to emit phase-1 line when entering phase 1 — it only happens at run start). So the reset should trigger whenever a phase-1 line arrives and the board isn't already showing phase 1 as the leading edge of the current run (i.e., phases[0].status === 'pending' or a fresh board). Actually simpler: any phase-1 line resets the board (phase 1 is the first line of every run; a phase-1 line mid-run can't meaningfully occur — and if the agent re-emits phase 1, restarting the board is still the honest view).

Careful with replay of the SAME run though: the fold is deterministic over the log; phase-1 line occurs exactly once per run in the log. If the agent (against the prompt) emits phase-1 twice, resetting twice is harmless.

So change the reset condition from:
```js
if (n === 1 && !state.running && phases[0].status !== 'pending') {
```
to:
```js
if (n === 1 && phases[0].status !== 'pending') {
```
Wait — but then within a single run, the FIRST phase-1 line: phases[0].status === 'pending' → no reset (nothing to reset) ✓. A phase-1 line after any prior run activity (completed OR abandoned-mid-failure) → reset ✓. A phase-1 line after a phase-1 line (duplicate emission in the same run): first line set phases[0] to active; second line resets the board (clears 2-8 back to pending, re-sets phase 1) — harmless and arguably correct (restart).

Hmm — one more scenario: the agent emits `[factory] phase 1/8 capture: ...` and then during phase 1 emits another phase-1 line ("phase 1/8 capture: done")? The prompt says one line on ENTER and one on LEAVE. A leave-line for phase 1 would be `[factory] phase 1/8 capture: acceptance criteria derived` — that's a phase-1 line arriving while phases[0] is active → my new logic resets the board (phases 2-8 pending — they already are; phase 1 active) → identical result. No harm.

But careful — `running` after reset: `final = pending` → running=true ✓ (the run continues).

Now — does this change alter the projection's `stateVersion`? The fold semantics change → per the practice ("bump stateVersion when fold semantics change") — since this plugin's projection has never successfully shipped a cached state (the UI never worked; but the projection COULD have been registered in the user's running host and cached rows written to the projection cache!). If the user ran /factory with the old bundle, the persisted projection cache may hold `factory` rows at stateVersion 1. Changing fold semantics without bumping stateVersion → stale cached state served. Bump to stateVersion: 2. The registry: "an incompatible version... throws" only when re-registered with a different version by another registrant; for the cache: "a row is usable iff its ver matches the live unit's stateVersion... an unusable row is discarded and its key refolds from init". So bumping to 2 discards old cached rows. Correct move: bump to 2.

Also update the README fold-semantics sentence to describe the fixed behavior ("a later phase-1 line starts a fresh run" — it already says that! The README documents the INTENDED behavior; the code just didn't deliver it. My fix aligns code with docs.)

Let me make the edit to index.js: change the condition and bump stateVersion 1 → 2. Also adjust the comment.

The current code:
```js
  let phases = state.phases.map((phase) => ({ ...phase }));
  // A phase-1 line after a finished run starts a fresh run.
  if (n === 1 && !state.running && phases[0].status !== 'pending') {
    phases = emptyProjectionState().phases;
  }
```
New:
```js
  let phases = state.phases.map((phase) => ({ ...phase }));
  // A phase-1 line always opens a run: reset every stale phase unless the
  // board is already pristine (first line of the very first run, or after a
  // reset). This also recovers a board abandoned mid-run by a failure.
  if (n === 1 && phases[0].status !== 'pending') {
    phases = emptyProjectionState().phases;
  }
```

And `stateVersion: 1` → `stateVersion: 2`.

Then re-run the smoke — and update the smoke expectation? The smoke test asserts the CORRECT semantic: `['active','pending']` — after the fix it should pass unchanged. Good — the smoke caught a genuine bug; the fix makes it pass.

